5417 true] [17592186092559 1074 "Let $T$ be the event that a chosen student obtained the top grade, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the student is from Group A,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the student is from Group B,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the student is from Group С.\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student obtained the top grade, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(T\\cap A)+P(T\\cap B)+P(T\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|B)P(B)+P(T|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $35%$ of students are in Group A, $35%$ are in Group B, and $30%$ are in Group C, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.35,\\,P(B)=0.35,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of the students in Group A, and $10%$ in Group B obtained the top grade, we have\n\n$$\nP(T|A)=0.2,\\,P(T|B)=0.1\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected student from any group obtained the top grade is $0.255,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=0.255.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(T|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|B)P(B)+P(T|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.255 & =(0.2)(0.35)+(0.1)(0.35)+P(T|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.255 & =0.07+0.035+0.3P(T|C) \\\\ 0.3P(T|C) & =0.15 \\\\ P(T|C) & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student from Group C obtained the top grade is $0.50.$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186096706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492872 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186096706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492926 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186096706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492928 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186096708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492924 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110377 1074 "The point $A$ corresponds to the number $x_1=-1$ while $B$ corresponds to $x_2=5$.\n\nTo find the number $x_m$ corresponding to the midpoint $M$, we use the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1 + 5}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=2$.\n\n![](images/07/07d9c940fbe2e5a14c5dc3fc1b46add8947f9ad0df7151f8693793cf70359688.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186110377 1074 "The point $A$ corresponds to the number $x_1=-1$ while $B$ corresponds to $x_2=5.$ \n\nTo find the number $x_m$ corresponding to the midpoint $M$, we use the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{m} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=2.$\n\n![](images/07/07d9c940fbe2e5a14c5dc3fc1b46add8947f9ad0df7151f8693793cf70359688.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110378 1071 "Given that $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$, what is the value of $x$?\n\n![](images/bf/bf08731ef8b88b55c1b3df41e58443beefb5e1a69b46757fbfb18c22d6c0b6b1.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186110378 1071 "Given that $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$, what is the value of $x?$\n\n![](images/bf/bf08731ef8b88b55c1b3df41e58443beefb5e1a69b46757fbfb18c22d6c0b6b1.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110378 1074 "Since $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$, the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is twice the length of the segment $\\overline{PM}$. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2PM &= PQ \\\\\n2(4x - 3) &= 5x + 9 \\\\\n8x - 6 &= 5x + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract $5x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x - 6 - 5x &= 5x + 9 - 5x \\\\\n3x - 6 &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $6$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 6 + 6 &= 9 + 6 \\\\\n3x &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3x}{3} &= \\frac{15}{3} \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186110378 1074 "Since $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$, the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is twice the length of the segment $\\overline{PM}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2PM & =PQ \\\\ 2(4x-3) & =5x+9 \\\\ 8x-6 & =5x+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract $5x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-6-5x & =5x+9-5x \\\\ 3x-6 & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $6$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-6+6 & =9+6 \\\\ 3x & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{15}{3} \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110379 1071 "The point $M$ is the midpoint of the line segment $\\overline{LN}$. What is the value of $x$ if $MN=4x+1$ centimeters and $LM=3x+4$ centimeters?" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186110379 1071 "The point $M$ is the midpoint of the line segment $\\overline{LN}.$ What is the value of $x$ if $MN=4x+1$ centimeters and $LM=3x+4$ centimeters?" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110379 1074 "We have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/60/60e50f27aef61d5a97f9165dab922fa25cd80c114a0b11bbfbcdfe4c0fdf90e9.png)\n\nSince $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{LN}$, the two segments $\\overline{LM}$ and $\\overline{MN}$ have the same length. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLM &= MN \\\\\n3x + 4 &= 4x + 1 \\\\\n3x + 4 - 3x &= 4x + 1 - 3x \\\\\n4 &= x + 1 \\\\\n4 - 1 &= x + 1 - 1 \\\\\n3 &= x \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx = 3\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\n." 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186110379 1074 "We have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/60/60e50f27aef61d5a97f9165dab922fa25cd80c114a0b11bbfbcdfe4c0fdf90e9.png)\n\nSince $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{LN}$, the two segments $\\overline{LM}$ and $\\overline{MN}$ have the same length. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LM & =MN \\\\ 3x+4 & =4x+1 \\\\ 3x+4-3x & =4x+1-3x \\\\ 4 & =x+1 \\\\ 4-1 & =x+1-1 \\\\ 3 & =x \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = 3\\,\\textrm{cm}$." 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110380 1071 "For the diagram depicted below, which number corresponds to the point $R$ if $M$ is the midpoint of the line segment $\\overline{RS}$?\n\n![](images/d4/d4b49c7832633378a281295850be72f2ff664475b79f9b692591411c2d9ed6c4.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186110380 1071 "For the diagram depicted below, which number corresponds to the point $R$ if $M$ is the midpoint of the line segment $\\overline{RS}?$\n\n![](images/d4/d4b49c7832633378a281295850be72f2ff664475b79f9b692591411c2d9ed6c4.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110380 1074 "From the picture, we can see that the point $S$ corresponds to the number $x_2=3$ while the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=1$.\n\nTo find the number $x_1$ corresponding to the point $R$, we first set up an equation using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2} \\\\\n1 &= \\frac{x_{1} + 3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x_1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= \\frac{x_{1} + 3}{2} \\\\\n2 \\cdot 1 &= x_{1} + 3 \\\\\n2 &= x_{1} + 3 \\\\\n2 - 3 &= x_{1} \\\\\n- 1 &= x_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $R$ corresponds to $x_1=-1$.\n\n![](images/f4/f4897cf174e575e4999ba9605c9ddd03b3820c63fd77a1d7f87fdcaac171e35b.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186110380 1074 "From the picture, we can see that the point $S$ corresponds to the number $x_2=3$ while the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=1.$ \n\nTo find the number $x_1$ corresponding to the point $R$, we first set up an equation using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{m} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+3}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot 1 & ={x}_{1}+3 \\\\ 2 & ={x}_{1}+3 \\\\ 2-3 & ={x}_{1} \\\\ -1 & ={x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $R$ corresponds to $x_1=-1.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4897cf174e575e4999ba9605c9ddd03b3820c63fd77a1d7f87fdcaac171e35b.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110383 1074 "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 15^{∘} &= 2x + 10^{∘} \\\\\nx - 15^{∘} &= 10^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 25^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x=25^\\circ$, we can substitute into one of the expressions to calculate the measure of the angles. Let's substitute into the expression $2x + 10^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n2x + 10^\\circ = 2 \\cdot 25^\\circ + 10^\\circ = 60^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, both angles have a measure of $60^\\circ$." 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186110383 1074 "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{15}^{∘} & =2x+{10}^{∘} \\\\ x-{15}^{∘} & ={10}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={25}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x=25^\\circ,$ we can substitute into one of the expressions to calculate the measure of the angles. Let's substitute into the expression $2x + 10^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n2x + 10^\\circ = 2 \\cdot 25^\\circ + 10^\\circ = 60^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, both angles have a measure of $60^\\circ.$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110384 1071 "In the figure below, $\\angle AOB$ is a straight angle. Determine $m \\angle AOD$.\n\n![](images/6e/6eac6c11dcc513cdf896cdfaa9650689af1bf58afd147783d0bb1ca61dd3a324.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186110384 1071 "In the figure below, $\\angle AOB$ is a straight angle. Determine $m \\angle AOD.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eac6c11dcc513cdf896cdfaa9650689af1bf58afd147783d0bb1ca61dd3a324.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110384 1074 "Since $\\angle AOB$ is straight,\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOB = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSo by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOC + m\\angle BOC = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions into the above, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5x + 100^{∘}) + 11x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n16x + 100^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n16x &= 80^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 5^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x=5^\\circ$, we can calculate $m \\angle COB$ as follows:\n\n$$\nm \\angle COB = 11(5^\\circ) = 55^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, since the angles $\\angle AOD$ and $\\angle COB$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm \\angle AOD = m \\angle COB = 55^\\circ\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186110384 1074 "Since $\\angle AOB$ is straight, $m\\angle AOB = 180^\\circ.$ So by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOC + m\\angle BOC = 180^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions into the above, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x+{100}^{∘})+11x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 16x+{100}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 16x & ={80}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x=5^\\circ,$ we can calculate $m \\angle COB$ as follows:\n\n$$\nm \\angle COB = 11(5^\\circ) = 55^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, since the angles $\\angle AOD$ and $\\angle COB$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm \\angle AOD = m \\angle COB = 55^\\circ\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110385 1071 "In the diagram shown below, it is known that $m \\angle 1 = 60^\\circ$ and $m \\angle 3 = 120^\\circ$. Determine the value of $m \\angle 2 + m \\angle 4$.\n\n![](images/1b/1bc662904a52724fac28dc180f70088592ff4da181930c6607b1bb590b3ec188.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186110385 1071 "In the diagram shown below, it is known that $m \\angle 1 = 60^\\circ$ and $m \\angle 3 = 120^\\circ.$ Determine the value of $m \\angle 2 + m \\angle 4.$\n\n![](images/1b/1bc662904a52724fac28dc180f70088592ff4da181930c6607b1bb590b3ec188.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110385 1074 "We have two pairs of vertical angles, and we know that vertical angles are congruent and therefore have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1=m∠2, \\\\ m∠3=m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $m \\angle 1 = 60^\\circ$ and $m \\angle 3 = 120^\\circ,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{60}^{∘}=m∠2, \\\\ {120}^{∘}=m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠2+m∠4 & ={60}^{∘}+{120}^{∘} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110385 1074 "We have two pairs of vertical angles, and we know that vertical angles are congruent and therefore have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\nm∠1 = m∠2,; m∠3 = m∠4\n$$\n\nWe are told that\n\n$$\nm \\angle 1 = 60^\\circ\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nm \\angle 3 = 120^\\circ\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\n60^{∘} = m∠2,; 120^{∘} = m∠4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠2 + m∠4 &= 60^{∘} + 120^{∘} \\\\\n&= 180^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186110436 1071 "Given the figure below, find the value of $x$.\n\n![](images/8b/8b89eec86675b2bebfda74ecf1da8e0b7980e5de6d3a52de84e93e64a4a62068.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186110436 1071 "Given the figure below, find the value of $x.$\n\n![](images/8b/8b89eec86675b2bebfda74ecf1da8e0b7980e5de6d3a52de84e93e64a4a62068.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110436 1074 "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical.\nSince vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 12^{∘} &= 54^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 42^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 21^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186110436 1074 "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. \nSince vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{12}^{∘} & ={54}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={42}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={21}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110547 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492833 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110547 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492869 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110744 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492887 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110744 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492890 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110745 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492838 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110745 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492904 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492826 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492927 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110747 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492856 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110747 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492865 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110749 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492834 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110749 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492880 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492861 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492885 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492842 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492898 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492925 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110835 1050 660.0 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186110862 1050 720.0 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186111835 1064 "The **midpoint** of a line segment is the point that splits it into two segments of equal length. For example, $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ede582389ad936b02a6074a3fb6543e730ae955baf9784147f133f9492a35708.png)\n\nThe midpoint splits the line segment into two congruent segments,\n\n$$\n\\overline{PM}\\cong \\overline{QM}\n$$\n\nIn addition, the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is twice as long as the segments $\\overline{PM}$ and $\\overline{QM}$:\n\n$$\nPQ = 2PM, \\qquad PQ = 2QM\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186111835 1064 "The **midpoint** of a line segment is the point that splits it into two segments of equal length. For example, $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ede582389ad936b02a6074a3fb6543e730ae955baf9784147f133f9492a35708.png)\n\nThe midpoint splits the line segment into two congruent segments,\n\n$$\n\\overline{PM}\\cong \\overline{QM}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is twice as long as the segments $\\overline{PM}$ and $\\overline{QM}$:\n\n$$\nPQ = 2PM, \\qquad PQ = 2QM\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186111838 1064 "When two lines meet at a point, they create two pairs of opposite angles. The angles in each pair are called **vertical angles**.\n\nVertical angles are *always* congruent.\n\n![](images/0d/0d5b2895ae18beb7242ef666af08e5b8e3490880de99aec8d993b02f31a9fcf4.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\angle 1$ and $\\angle 3$ are vertical angles and are therefore congruent.\n\nLikewise, $\\angle 2$ and $\\angle 4$ are vertical angles and are therefore congruent.\n\nSo, we have $\\angle 1 \\cong \\angle 3$ and $\\angle 2 \\cong \\angle 4$." 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186111838 1064 "When two lines meet at a point, they create two pairs of opposite angles. The angles in each pair are called **vertical angles**.\n\nVertical angles are *always* congruent.\n\n![](images/0d/0d5b2895ae18beb7242ef666af08e5b8e3490880de99aec8d993b02f31a9fcf4.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\angle 1$ and $\\angle 3$ are vertical angles and are therefore congruent. \n\nLikewise, $\\angle 2$ and $\\angle 4$ are vertical angles and are therefore congruent. \n\nSo, we have $\\angle 1 \\cong \\angle 3$ and $\\angle 2 \\cong \\angle 4.$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186111857 1064 "Let's find the midpoint $M$ of the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ on a number line, if $A$ corresponds to $x_1= 3$ and $B$ corresponds to $x_2=7$, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/11/115797e11eb3e434d5faefe412a8cefea216b6190436d408d199ac31d0da7210.png)\n\nThe midpoint of a line segment splits it into two segments of equal length.\nTo find the number $x_m$ corresponding to $M$, we use the following **midpoint formula**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x_1=3$ and $x_2=7$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{m} &= \\frac{3 + 7}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{2} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=5$.\n\n![](images/fc/fc0a47734756e12ec117db92d8f0273ae564c03ead88ce86eac82005be6feaf4.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186111857 1064 "Let's find the midpoint $M$ of the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ on a number line, if $A$ corresponds to $x_1= 3$ and $B$ corresponds to $x_2=7,$ as depicted below.\n\n![](images/11/115797e11eb3e434d5faefe412a8cefea216b6190436d408d199ac31d0da7210.png)\n\n \n\nThe midpoint of a line segment splits it into two segments of equal length.\nTo find the number $x_m$ corresponding to $M,$ we use the following **midpoint formula**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{m} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x_1=3$ and $x_2=7$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{m} & =\\frac{3+7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=5.$\n\n![](images/fc/fc0a47734756e12ec117db92d8f0273ae564c03ead88ce86eac82005be6feaf4.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186113917 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113919 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186113917 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113920 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186113917 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113921 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186113917 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113922 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186113917 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492423 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186113917 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492497 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186113917 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492510 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186113917 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492582 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186113917 1078 #edb/ref 17592186492497 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186115242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492874 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186115299 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492840 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186115830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186115830 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3z=-2 \\\\ x+y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186115830 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n${x + 3z =-2; x + y = 0$.\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186115830 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+3z=-2 \\\\ y-3z=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2-3z \\\\ y=2+3z \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the latter can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2-3z \\\\ 2+3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186133816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492825 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186138368 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492879 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186138421 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492848 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186138423 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492909 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186146810 1075 #edb/ref 17592186146811 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186146810 1075 #edb/ref 17592186146812 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186146810 1075 #edb/ref 17592186146813 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186146810 1075 #edb/ref 17592186146814 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186146810 1075 #edb/ref 17592186146815 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186146810 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492387 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186146810 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492512 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186146810 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492627 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186146810 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492656 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186146810 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492676 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186146810 1078 #edb/ref 17592186492676 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186147415 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492897 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186147446 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492860 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186148176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186148176 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186148176 1071 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186148176 1074 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing our equation with the above, we can see that the only equation that represents damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+4y=0.\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186160886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186160887 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186160886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186160888 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186160886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186160889 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186160886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186160890 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186160886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186160891 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186160886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492430 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186160886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492519 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186160886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492606 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186160886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492628 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186160886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492702 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186160886 1078 #edb/ref 17592186492606 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186161780 1071 "![](images/1d/1d054c7daaaeefd4cdf820d49d04d6a0bd544f09e356b859b6291ff7d4588e19.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, find the third column of the matrix $P.$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186161780 1071 "![](images/ad/ad112d0eeeea5affe9a7515bbe6e4b36fabc1f069b2152b7d29313936af644a5.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, find the third column of the matrix $P.$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186161780 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=0,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ and $\\lambda_3=4.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_3=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_3=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector \n\n![](images/1b/1b5ddaab68e99fda2cf15dbdcaaf5dfc8caca6b24ee6ea7b4d87e8fd49245c6e.png)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v}_3 \\| = \\sqrt{6}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_3$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/7c/7c063e9aa7d59bf0f7b30cb311452508583b81d349076aa198cae059c038bb02.png)" 13194139535300 17592186045417 false] [17592186161780 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1